  "response": "The scale of the episode is not in dispute: US CPI inflation peaked at 9.1 percent year-over-year in June 2022, a peak brought about by the pandemic-era supply disruptions that raised US consumer prices during 2021\u20132022. By contrast, a second figure places the peak higher, holding that US CPI inflation peaked at 11.2 percent year-over-year in 2022. Whichever magnitude anchors the analysis, the contested question concerns causes rather than size. Economists have sought to determine whether the surge originated chiefly in the productive capacity of the economy or in the spending power injected into it, because the answer governs how much of the burden of disinflation monetary policy should have carried.\n\nThe competing explanations need not exclude one another. At least one of two mechanisms was at work, perhaps both: pandemic-era supply disruptions raised US consumer prices during 2021\u20132022, and excess demand from fiscal and monetary stimulus raised US consumer prices during the same period. The supply channel has the more direct empirical signature. The New York Fed's Global Supply Chain Pressure Index reached a record high in late 2021, and that reading indicates that disruptions in shipping, manufacturing inputs and delivery times were pushing costs upward across a wide range of goods. The demand channel is harder to observe directly, since stimulus works through household balance sheets and spending decisions rather than through a single measurable bottleneck.\n\nAttempts to apportion the surge between these channels therefore turned to statistical decomposition. Adam Shapiro of the San Francisco Fed developed a method to split PCE inflation into supply-driven and demand-driven components, classifying each category of spending according to whether its prices and quantities moved together or in opposite directions. Even though the demand channel was plainly operating, Shapiro's decomposition attributed a larger share of mid-2022 PCE inflation to supply factors than to demand factors. The alternative reading is that the decomposition attributed a share of mid-2022 PCE inflation to demand factors at least as large as its share to supply factors. These two characterizations cannot both be true, and one of them must hold: either supply dominated in mid-2022, or demand matched or exceeded it. A San Francisco Fed study by Jord\u00e0 and coauthors settles the tension between the demand channel and the supply-heavy decomposition, having determined that US fiscal support added about 3 percentage points to inflation by late 2021. That fiscal impulse caused the demand-driven component of the decomposition to grow until it was at least as large as the supply-driven one.\n\nA complementary approach models wage and price dynamics jointly rather than classifying spending categories. Bernanke and Blanchard published their decomposition of the inflation surge in 2019. In it, they found that the contribution of labor market tightness to inflation grew over time, with vacancies relative to unemployment feeding into wages and then into prices with a lag. Their 2019 decomposition confirms the central conclusion they drew: price shocks to energy, food, and specific sectors, rather than overheated labor markets, drove most of the initial US inflation surge. On this account, the demand-side pressure transmitted through employment was real but secondary at the outset, gaining importance only as the initial shocks receded.\n\nBy contrast, the Federal Reserve began raising the federal funds rate in March 2022, which came well before it raised the federal funds rate target range to 5.25\u20135.50 percent in July 2023. In other words, in July 2023 the FOMC set its policy rate target at between 5\u00bc and 5\u00bd percent. The tightening cycle thus extended over roughly sixteen months and carried the policy rate to its highest level in more than two decades. The scholarly adjudication has accordingly converged on a layered answer rather than a single verdict. Supply shocks set the surge in motion, as the supply-chain index and the sectoral price evidence show, while fiscal support and labor market tightness sustained and broadened it. The weight assigned to each depends on the period examined and on the identifying assumptions of the method employed.",
